# cuttr speaker labels — mia-take-1
#
# Who says each line of a real five-minute take: a German interview with a
# child, Mia, and at least two adults. Labelled by hand, by reading. An
# interview alternates question and answer and the text says which is which.
#
# THE WORDS ARE NOT HERE, AND MUST NOT BE. This repository is public and the
# take is a seven-year-old's family interview. A time and a name are the shape
# of a conversation; the transcript is its content, and the content stays on
# the machine that recorded it. The words live beside the take, wherever that
# is, and the tests that need them are gated on CUTTR_FOOTAGE and skip without
# it — see Tests/CuttrKitTests/GroundTruthTests.swift and docs/speakers.md.
#
# Matched to a take by TIME, not by line number, so that a change to how lines
# are divided does not silently shift every label by one. Each row is the start
# of a line, on the video's clock, and who said it.
#
# Two labels, not three. There are certainly two adults in the room — the
# exchange from 00:32 to 00:54 is one of them talking to the other about where
# to cut — but which adult says which of those lines cannot be read off the
# text, only heard. Reading is the method that was used, so reading is what
# these claim. Anybody who listens can split `interviewer` in two by editing
# this file and nothing else.
#
# Read but not certain, four lines: 00:10.567, 00:20.947, 00:30.007 and
# 00:35.167. They are 6% of the take and are labelled as read, so a method that
# gets them wrong is not being marked down in secret.
#
# 68 lines: 26 interviewer, 42 mia. Always answering `mia` scores 61.8%, which
# is the number any method has to beat before it is worth switching on.
#
# at           who
